GUI modernization, Phase 3: useRecord.ts rewritten to actually consume
cursor pagination and conditional GET (ADR 0005), closing P1-01 - the
Tauri bridge needed real changes first, since headers and cancellation
did not exist in either direction before this.

## 1. The Tauri bridge had no header passthrough or cancellation at all

Checked before writing any TypeScript: `agent::get(endpoint, path)`
(`desktop/src-tauri/src/agent.rs`) sent only two hard-coded headers and
returned status+body, nothing else - no way for the frontend to read an
`ETag` or send `If-None-Match`, and no way to interrupt an in-flight
named-pipe/socket read once started. `useRecord.ts`'s own `generation`
counter already stopped a stale answer from being *applied*, but the
underlying IPC call itself always ran to completion regardless - "ignored
on arrival," not "actually cancelled," which the execution order's own
wording specifically distinguishes.

Fixed properly, not worked around:

- `agent.rs`: `get()` takes `extra_headers` and returns lower-cased
  response `headers` alongside status/body.
- `lib.rs`: `agent_get` takes optional `headers` (request) and returns
  `headers` (response); takes an optional `request_id` that registers a
  `tokio::sync::Notify` in a new `AgentState` (managed via `.manage()`);
  a new `agent_cancel(request_id)` command wakes it. The actual race is
  `race_cancellable`, pulled out as its own function specifically so it
  could be unit tested without spinning up a Tauri app:

    $ cargo test --lib --manifest-path desktop/src-tauri/Cargo.toml
    test tests::race_cancellable_returns_the_futures_own_result_when_never_cancelled ... ok
    test tests::race_cancellable_returns_the_cancelled_sentinel_when_notified_first ... ok

  The second test races `std::future::pending()` (a future that
  mathematically never resolves on its own) against a pre-triggered
  notification - if the test returns anything at all, the cancellation
  path is what returned it, ruling out a false pass from the "real" work
  coincidentally finishing first.

  `cargo build`/`cargo test --lib` (10/10, up from 8) both clean;
  `cargo clippy --lib`: 0 warnings.

## 2. TypeScript bridge (`desktop/src/data/tauri.ts`)

`agentGetRaw<T>` alongside the existing `agentGet`: takes `{headers,
requestId}`, returns `{status, data, headers}` with `data: null` on a 304
(a real answer with nothing to parse, not an error). A Rust
`Err("__cancelled__")` (Tauri's rejection for a cancelled command) is
caught and re-thrown as a `CancelledError` the caller can distinguish from
a genuine transport failure.

**A real bug found while writing this, before any test ran**: the fake
recorder fixture in the existing `useRecord.test.tsx` (predates this work)
returns `{status, body}` with no `headers` field at all. `agentGetRaw`
read `resp.headers.etag` unconditionally, which is a `TypeError` on
`undefined`, not a missing value - confirmed by running the existing
suite and watching a passing test start failing. Fixed by normalizing
`resp.headers ?? {}` once, at the point the Rust response is received,
rather than trusting every call site to guard against a header map that
happens to be absent.

## 3. `useRecord.ts` rewrite

`fetchLivePoll(endpoint, prev: LiveState, generation)` replaces the old
`fetchLive` internally: `since=` for the very first call or whenever a
cursor was never obtained; `cursor=<prev's next_cursor>` otherwise.
`mergeById` folds a repeat's grown count into the existing array position
instead of duplicating it (matching the backend's own fold semantics) or
simply appending everything blindly. `capabilities`/`rules` send
`If-None-Match` once an `ETag` is known; a 304 keeps what was already held
rather than being treated as an empty answer. `cancelPoll(prevGeneration)`
is called before every new poll starts, issuing real `agent_cancel` calls
for whichever of the previous poll's five sub-requests are still running.

**A second real bug found while writing this**, not by a test failing but
by re-reading my own code before running anything: the scheduling
`useEffect`'s `scheduleNext` closure read `state.status` to decide whether
to use the normal interval or the backoff delay - but `state` is
deliberately not in that effect's dependency array (re-running the whole
effect on every state change would tear down and restart the polling
timer constantly), so `scheduleNext` would only ever see the `status` from
the render that created it, never a later poll's actual outcome. Replaced
with `consecutiveFailures`, a ref (always current, immune to this class of
staleness by construction), incremented on failure and reset on success.

## 4. Verified end-to-end at the frontend level

`desktop/src/data/useRecord.cursor.test.tsx` (new) - a fake recorder that
actually behaves like the real API, not a fixed canned response:

    $ npx vitest run src/data/useRecord.cursor.test.tsx --reporter=verbose
    ✓ uses since= on the first load and cursor= on the next poll, merging
      a folded event's new count instead of duplicating it
    ✓ sends If-None-Match on the second poll and keeps the existing
      rules/capabilities on a 304 instead of clearing them
    ✓ cancels the previous poll's in-flight requests when settings change
      before it resolves

The first test asserts the exact second-call path string
(`/v1/events?cursor=CURSOR-1&limit=5000`) rather than merely "a request
was made," and asserts the merged array's *order and contents*
(event 01A's count grown in place at index 0, new event 01B appended after
it) - the specific claim this whole rewrite exists to make true, not an
approximation of it.

